.TH PMASK 1 "2026" "pmask 0.1.0-beta.1" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
pmask \- run a process tree with a redacted /proc entry
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B pmask
[\fIOPTIONS\fR] [\fB\-\-\fR] \fICOMMAND\fR [\fIARGS\fR...]
.br
.B pmask
[\fIOPTIONS\fR] \fB\-\-args\-from\-fd\fR \fIN\fR
.br
.B pmask
[\fIOPTIONS\fR] \fB\-\-image\fR \fIPATH\fR [\fIARGS\fR...]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B pmask
is a ptrace supervisor.
It runs a program and every process that program spawns.
Each process shows a presentation label in
.BR /proc/ pid /cmdline
and
.BR /proc/ pid /comm
instead of its real command line and process name.
Every other local account sees the label.
The program itself receives its real arguments and environment.
.PP
A process is syscall-supervised until its first exec completes.
At the execve entry stop the supervisor rewrites the argument and
environment pointer arrays of the old image.
The kernel then copies the label and empty strings, so the real command
line never exists in the new image.
At the exec completion stop the supervisor writes the real strings into
an anonymous mapping injected inside the tracee and repoints the
arrays.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.BR \-l ", " \-\-label " \fINAME\fR"
Presentation label.
The value
.B \-
gives a blank label (one space).
The label must not contain NUL, LF or CR.
Default: the program name, with all arguments removed.
.TP
.B \-\-label\-empty
Empty label.
.TP
.BI \-\-argv0 " NAME"
argv[0] of the program.
Default: the command exactly as typed.
.TP
.BI \-\-comm " NAME"
Process name for
.BR top ,
at most 15 bytes.
Default: the label cut to 15 bytes.
.TP
.B \-\-keep\-env
Leave
.B /proc/\fIpid\fB/environ
visible.
.TP
.BI \-\-keep\-env= NAME[,NAME...]
Keep only the named variables visible.
The inline form is required.
Repeatable.
.TP
.BI \-\-env " K=V"
Add or replace one variable.
Repeatable.
.TP
.BI \-\-env\-file " PATH"
Read variables from a file: one KEY=VALUE per line, # starts a comment
line, blank lines are skipped, values are taken as written.
Repeatable.
.TP
.BI \-\-args\-from\-fd " N"
Read the command from file descriptor N as a NUL-separated vector:
program first, then arguments.
The vector replaces
.IR COMMAND ,
so no command may follow the options.
It is capped at 8 MiB; a larger vector is refused without buffering it
all.
.TP
.BI \-\-on\-exit " POLICY"
What happens to descendants still running when the command exits:
.B detach
(default; each settled descendant is detached and keeps the label),
.B wait
(supervise until the whole tree exits) or
.B kill
(send SIGKILL).
.TP
.B \-\-no\-follow
Redact only the direct child.
Descendants are traced but not redacted.
.TP
.B \-\-no\-exit\-kill
Do not set PTRACE_O_EXITKILL: the tree survives if the supervisor dies.
.TP
.B \-\-no\-dumpable
Set PR_SET_DUMPABLE=0 in every process of the tree.
Same-uid observers then cannot read environ, maps, exe or fd.
Core dumps and debugger attach stop working.
.TP
.B \-\-no\-new\-privs
Set no_new_privs before exec.
Set-uid and file-capability programs gain nothing.
.TP
.B \-\-no\-exact
Keep the original cmdline byte extent: the label is written over the
real arguments, but
.BR /proc/\fIpid\fB/stat
still reports how many bytes they took.
By default the kernel's argv and environment windows are moved onto the
label with
.BR prctl(PR_SET_MM_MAP) ,
so cmdline is exactly the label and environ is empty (or keeps the
variables named by
.BR \-\-keep\-env ).
.TP
.B \-\-detach
Detach each process once its exec has settled, so
.B TracerPid
reads 0 for the payload.
This is a coverage trade: a detached process and everything it forks
later run unredacted, and the session summary counts each detach.
.TP
.B \-\-watch\-rename
Revert a later prctl(PR_SET_NAME) to the label.
Installs a seccomp filter that traps that call only.
Implies
.BR \-\-no\-new\-privs .
If the filter cannot be installed, the run exits 126 naming the failure
instead of running without the promise.
.TP
.B \-\-rexec
Exec the program from a sealed memfd, so
.B /proc/\fIpid\fB/exe
names the label.
The child tries the carriers in order:
.BR execveat ( AT_EMPTY_PATH ),
then
.BR /proc/self/fd/ N
with an ordinary
.BR execve ,
then a temporary file on a mount that allows execution.
Every failed step is reported and counted.
Without
.B \-\-rexec\-allow\-disk
a refused image ends the child with 126 (127 when the image is
missing) instead of running the on-disk file.
.TP
.B \-\-rexec\-allow\-disk
Add the on-disk path as the explicit last resort of the
.B \-\-rexec
chain.
The fallback is reported and counted; it is never automatic.
.TP
.BI \-\-image " PATH"
Read the program bytes from PATH and exec them from a sealed image.
PATH is opened as given, not searched in PATH.
.B \-\-image \-
reads standard input to end of file.
Only ELF images work; a script needs an interpreter that opens a path.
Implies
.BR \-\-rexec .
.TP
.BI \-\-image\-from\-fd " N"
Read the program bytes from descriptor N.
Implies
.BR \-\-rexec .
.TP
.B \-\-exec\-once
Reuse one sealed image per distinct program, keyed by the resolved path
and its mtime, across the execs of the session.
Later execs use a guarded temporary-file image, because the supervisor
is non-dumpable and the tracee cannot read its descriptors.
Implies
.BR \-\-rexec .
.TP
.B \-\-hugetlb
Ask for a hugetlbfs-backed image and report the backing it got.
When huge pages are unavailable the run reports it and uses an ordinary
memfd.
Implies
.BR \-\-rexec .
.TP
.B \-\-unshare
Run in new user, pid and mount namespaces with a private /proc, when
the kernel allows it.
Prints one line and continues without it otherwise.
.TP
.BI \-\-scope\-name " NAME"
Re-exec once through
.BR "systemd-run \-\-scope" .
The unit name is visible in
.BR /proc/\fIpid\fB/cgroup .
.TP
.BR \-q ", " \-\-quiet
Suppress diagnostics, including the session summary.
.TP
.BR \-v ", " \-\-verbose
Trace every redaction event to stderr.
.TP
.BR \-V ", " \-\-version
Print the version.
.TP
.BR \-h ", " \-\-help
Print the help text.
.SH SECRET SIDE
These options and the subcommands below work even where ptrace is
refused, except
.BR \-\-secret\-close\-on\-exec ,
which needs the tracer and is refused in plain and isolate modes.
.TP
.BI \-\-secret " N=SRC"
Deliver a secret on descriptor 3, 4, ...; the child's environment
variable
.B PMASK_SECRET_<NAME>=<fd>
holds the descriptor number.
SRC is one of
.BR file: PATH ,
.BR env: VAR ,
.BR agent: NAME ,
.B stdin
or
.BR literal: VAL .
Repeatable.
A
.B literal:
or
.B env:
source still sits in pmask's own command line or environment, where
/proc shows it; the value is absent from the child's /proc entry.
The delivered descriptor survives exec, so every descendant inherits it
and a same-uid peer can read /proc/<pid>/fd/N, unless
.B \-\-secret\-close\-on\-exec
is given.
.TP
.BI \-\-socket " PATH"
Secret-agent socket for
.B agent: NAME
sources.
.TP
.BI \-\-isolate "[=LIST]"
Run the payload in fresh namespaces:
.B pid,net,tmp
|
.B all
|
.B none
(default all).
.TP
.B \-\-redact
Ask for /proc redaction explicitly (the default).
With
.B \-\-isolate
it is the composed spelling of
.BR \-\-unshare :
the label and a private pid namespace and procfs in one run.
.TP
.B \-\-no\-redact
Never touch /proc metadata, even where ptrace works.
.TP
.B \-\-allow\-argv\-secret
Keep a
.B literal:
or
.B env:
secret source in a mode that cannot redact /proc, and print the
warning that names the disclosure, instead of refusing the run.
.TP
.B \-\-secret\-burn
Seal the delivered descriptors without
.BR F_SEAL_SHRINK ,
so the reader can truncate a value to zero once it has used it, and
cannot grow or rewrite it afterwards.
.TP
.B \-\-secret\-close\-on\-exec
The tracer closes each delivered descriptor in each direct child of the
payload at that child's fork.
The payload keeps the descriptor and reads its value after its own
exec.
The guarantee is for an ordinary fork/exec tree: a process that shares
the payload's descriptor table
.RB ( clone(CLONE_FILES)
without
.BR CLONE_THREAD )
cannot be closed without removing the payload's copy, so the run
reports a degradation and the session counts it.
With
.B \-\-detach
the policy cannot be enforced below a detached tracee, and the run
prints one warning.
.TP
.B \-\-audit
Print one residue row per tracee at each exec completion, then a residue
count naming the interfaces the tree still discloses.
A field that cannot be read prints
.BR "not reachable" ,
never a zero.
Needs the redaction mode; a plain or isolate run refuses the flag.
.TP
.BI \-\-report " json"
Print the run as one JSON object on stdout.
.B text
and
.B plain
select the default human-readable output.
The report is produced by the secret-side runner, so it needs
.BR \-\-secret ,
.B \-\-isolate
or
.BR \-\-no\-redact .
A plain mask run and a
.B \-\-daemon
job refuse the flag with exit status 255.
.SH SUBCOMMANDS
.TP
.B run " [OPTIONS] [--] CMD"
The same parser as the default mode; accepts every option above.
.TP
.B read " NAME"
Print a secret that
.B run
delivered to this process.
One read per descriptor: the descriptor offset is shared with every
inheritor.
.TP
.B store " <sub>"
The encrypted store:
.BR init ,
.BR set\ NAME ,
.BR get\ NAME ,
.BR list ,
.BR rm\ NAME ,
.BR path .
Options:
.BI \-\-store " PATH"
(default $XDG_DATA_HOME/pmask/store.enc),
.BI \-\-passphrase\-file " P"
and, for
.BR "set" ,
.BI \-\-from\-file " P"
(the value then comes from P instead of stdin).
.TP
.B agent " [OPTIONS]"
Hold the store unlocked and serve it over a socket.
Options:
.BI \-\-store " PATH,"
.BI \-\-socket " PATH,"
.BI \-\-passphrase\-file " P"
and
.BI \-\-allow " EXE"
(repeatable; an exact-path allow-list).
.TP
.B get " NAME [--socket P]"
Ask a running agent for one secret.
.TP
.B probe " [--json]"
What this host allows, and what that means;\~\-\-json is machine-readable.
.TP
.B doctor " [--json] [--require LIST]"
The store, the agent and the verdict, in one report.
LIST names the guarantees the caller depends on: redact, exact,
isolate, secret\-mem, mlock, no\-new\-privs, non\-dumpable, landlock.
Exit status is 0 when every requested guarantee is active, 3 when one
is degraded and 4 when one is unavailable.
.TP
.BR help ", " version
Print the help text or the version.
.SH ALIASES
.BR \-a " (= \-\-label), " \-a\-
(= \-l\-),
.BR \-E " (= \-\-keep\-env), " \-F " (= \-\-no\-follow), " \-K " (= \-\-no\-exit\-kill)."
.B \-f
and
.B \-c
are accepted and have no effect.
.SH ENVIRONMENT
.TP
.B PMASK_NO_SELFMASK
Do not redact the supervisor's own /proc entry.
Use only for debugging: the supervisor then shows its real command line,
which contains the secrets it was given.
.SH LABEL LIMITS
The label is cut in three places.
.B comm
keeps 15 bytes.
The exec entry scratch keeps 254 bytes of the label until the exec
completes.
The in-place redaction, used when the kernel window is not moved, cuts
the label to the span of the argument area the kernel copied.
.PP
By default the completion path builds a block with the full label and
moves the kernel's argv window onto it, so a settled process shows the
whole label.
.B \-\-no\-exact
and a kernel that refuses
.BR PR_SET_MM_MAP
leave the window where it was, and the label stays cut to that span.
A warning is printed once, unless
.BR \-\-quiet ,
when the label is longer than 254 bytes.
.SH SESSION SUMMARY
When any exec was degraded, the session prints one line:
.PP
.RS
pmask: \fIN\fR of \fIM\fR execs degraded (\fIreasons\fR)
.RE
.PP
Every entry is a degradation, not a disclosure: the exec was redacted,
or one part of the treatment was skipped.
.B unredacted
is the entry where the real strings may have been visible.
The reasons are
.BR script ,
.BR reexec ,
.BR unredacted ,
.BR comm\-not\-set
and
.BR foreign\-arch ,
with a count when a reason occurred more than once.
.PP
Three further lines, each prefixed with
.BR pmask: ,
appear when they apply:
.BI "N of M execs kept the kernel's argv and environ windows" " (PR_SET_MM_MAP refused),"
.BI "N tracee(s) left detached after their exec" " (--detach: their later execs are not redacted),"
and
.B "N secret descriptor(s) could not be closed in a descendant."
.B \-\-quiet
suppresses all of them.
.SH DAEMON MODE
With
.BI \-\-daemon " PATH"
the job runs inside a
.BR pmaskd (8).
The daemon owns the environment and the masking policy.
Mask options other than
.B \-\-label
and
.B \-\-label\-empty
have no effect there and produce a warning.
.BR \-\-secret ,
.B \-\-isolate
and
.B \-\-no\-redact
are refused with exit status 255, because the daemon cannot honour
them.
.B \-\-report
is refused with exit status 255; the daemon returns the exit status only.
.SH EXIT STATUS
The exit status of the supervised command.
A command killed by a signal makes
.B pmask
die by the same signal.
Usage errors exit 255.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR pmaskd (8)
